# Hammer - pianos and their relatives, from a hammer and what it strikes.
#
# What each key of the grand is comes from a recorded concert grand,
# measured (tools/hammer_reference): how stiff its strings are, how long
# each part of a note rings, how far the first fast decay falls before the
# slow one takes over, how bright the strike is and how that changes with
# velocity. Init is that grand. The upright, the honky-tonk, the electric
# grand, the tine and the reed are measured the same way against their own
# recordings; the fortepiano, the tangent keyboard, the celesta, the toy
# piano, the dulcimer and the cimbalom are built from what's written about
# them.
#
# Seventy patches in seven families, plainest first:
#
#   grand     Init, size, felt, lid, mics and rooms, a rolled tremolo (12)
#   upright   uprights and honky-tonks, felt, tacks, out of tune (9)
#   electric  tine, reed and electric grand: drive, tremolo, phaser (13)
#   keys      tangent and fortepiano, the moderator, a filter (8)
#   bells     celesta and toy piano, a room, an echo, detuned (9)
#   strings   dulcimer and cimbalom, struck and in tremolo (9)
#   strange   preparations, on every key or only some of them (10)
#
# The patches move each instrument away from itself: the size of the
# piano, the felt, where you listen from, how old it is, what's been put on
# its strings. `roll` strikes a held note again and again, as a dulcimer or
# cimbalom player does; `duplex` lets the strings' free ends ring along
# faintly, and is up on the brighter grands and uprights. `clang` is the
# metal ring of a hard blow, up on the honky-tonks, the tack piano and the
# bright uprights, where Bright brings more of it. `tension` stiffens the
# middle and top strings: the baby grand, the tack piano and bright
# upright a little, Gamelan Keys and Stiff Wire more.
#
# Each patch plays the `piano` phrase (the pedal changed every bar, then
# three notes alone) or, for the bells, the `bell` phrase, inside its own
# range. Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Hammer`
# lists them. A patch names only what it changes. Every patch sits at -33.5
# on `loud` at its own note, within about a decibel, but for Rubber Mutes
# (-35.5), Upright Piano and Fortepiano Moderator (-34.3) and Bright Grand
# (-32.8). A prepared key is louder or quieter than its neighbours by up to
# 4 dB, so the prepared patches are measured on a prepared key.
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are
# numbered as in Hammer.h's MacroDest: #2 age, #3 hardness, #4 hardkey,
# #7 felt, #8 tacks, #10 sustain, #12 tone, #14 stiffness, #15 stretch,
# #16 unison, #19 dampers, #24 sympathy, #25 duplex, #27 board, #28 lid,
# #29 tail, #30 width, #31 pickup, #32 offset, #33 tonebar, #34 mute,
# #35 drive, #36 tremolo, #37 tremrate, #38 tremwide, #39 prep at,
# #40 prepamt, #41 volume, #43 roll, #45 tension, #46 clang. Where a macro at full moves the level
# by more than 1.5 dB, a volume route takes it back out, as far as the
# volume knob goes: Tremolo on Tine Autopan and Phase Tine stays 2-3 dB
# down and Short on Wind-Up Celesta and Broken Toy 2 dB down, as their
# volumes are at the top. Most of a piano is set when a key is struck, so
# the felt, the strings and the dampers move from the next note.
#
# What is still wrong:
#   - The grand's knee (how far the prompt sound falls before the
#     aftersound) is about 11 dB from the recording's across the keyboard:
#     too shallow in the bass, too deep at the top.
#   - The soft reed is purer than its recording above the 2nd harmonic.
#   - The fortepiano, tangent, celesta, toy, dulcimer and cimbalom have no
#     recording behind them, only the literature and an ear.

machine Hammer
role    piano

# --- grand -------------------------------------------------------------------

patch Init family=grand note=60 range=21..108

patch "Mellow Grand" family=grand note=60 range=21..108
